==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{m|non|akr}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*akraz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₂éǵros||field}} whence also the Danish {{m|da|ager}}, Faroese {{m|fo|akur}}, Swedish {{m|sv|åker}} and Norwegian {{m|no|åker}}.

Germanic cognates include Old English {{m|ang|æcer}} (English {{m|en|acre}}), Old Frisian {{m|ofs|ekker}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|akkar}} (Dutch {{m|nl|akker}}), Old High German {{m|goh|ackar}} (German {{m|de|Acker}}) and Gothic {{m|got|𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍃}}. Other cognates include Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ἀγρός||sc=polytonic}}, Mycenaean Greek {{m|gmy|𐀀𐀒𐀫}}, Latin {{m|la|ager}} (Spanish {{m|es|agreste}}), Umbrian {{m|xum|ager}}, Old Armenian {{m|xcl|արտ}} and Sanskrit {{m|sa|अज्र|tr=ájra}}.

===Pronunciation===
